<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catamarca Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Catamarca is a province in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, with its capital at San Fernando del Valle de Catamarca, founded in 1683. Andean ranges and dry valleys cover much of it, and it was home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaguita">Diaguita</a> peoples who resisted Spanish rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchaqui-wars">Calchaquí Wars</a>. It was part of the short-lived Republic of Tucumán before becoming an autonomous province in 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catamarca-province.jpg" alt="Catamarca Province" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Camino a Campo de Piedra Pomez en Catamarca · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CATAMARCA_318_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Catamarca lies in the northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and covers 102,602 square kilometres. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, Tucumán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-del-estero">Santiago del Estero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a>, and to the west the country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Eighty percent of the territory is covered by mountains, which fall into four systems: the Pampean sierras in the east and centre, the Narváez-Cerro Negro-Famatina system in the west, a cordilleran transition area at the western extreme, and the Puna, an elevated portion in the northwest.</p><p>The province is semi-arid, and the scarce water determines where people live: agriculture is concentrated in pockets and valleys between the mountains, and in the east the population gathers around watercourses from which canals and ditches carry the water. The Puna in the extreme west has a desert climate with very low precipitation, and snow cover is permanent at the highest peaks of the Sierra del Aconquija.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Spanish conquest most of what is now Catamarca was inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaguita">Diaguita</a> people, among them the fierce Calchaquí. In 1558 Juan Pérez de Zurita founded Londres de la Nueva Inglaterra, but it was constantly attacked by indigenous people, its population stayed small, and it was re-founded several times at new sites with new names. The sixth foundation, by Fernando de Mendoza Mate de Luna on 5 July 1683, bore the name San Fernando del Valle de Catamarca. The name of the province is believed to come either from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> cata, slope, and marca, fortress, or from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> catán, small, and marca, town.</p><p>When the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> was created in 1776, Catamarca became a subintendencia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a> intendency. In 1821 the province claimed its autonomy and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda">Nicolás Avellaneda</a> y Tula as its first governor. The mountains kept it isolated from the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> until 1888, when the railways first reached it. Its valleys and dry climate attracted immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, who found it reminiscent of the orchard-lined mountain valleys they had left.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Catamarca is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> with its capital at San Fernando del Valle de Catamarca, usually shortened to Catamarca. Its population was 429,556 at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and other important cities are Andalgalá, Tinogasta and Belén. The province is divided into sixteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>.</p><p>Mining is important in its economy, notably the Bajo de la Alumbrera copper and gold mine. Among its sights are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antofagasta">Antofagasta</a> de la Sierra, the San Francisco Pass, the archaeological park Las Huellas del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> and prehistoric petroglyphs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catamarca Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It claimed the provinces of Tucumán, <b>Catamarca</b> and Santiago del Estero, and adopted a constitution that year.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-tucuman">Republic of Tucumán</a></li></ul></section>
